      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Cardiac allograft vascular disease is (CAVD) the most important cause of death following heart transplantation (HTX). Although in the past, researchers focused predominantly on mechanisms of endothelial injury, the possible role of recipient-related and genetically determined factors has not been studied in detail."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Stimulated by recent observations in native coronary artery disease, we analyzed the potential impact of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) polymorphism (insertion/deletion [I/D], intron 16) on development and progression of CAVD. We characterized genotype in 146 patients 1 to 12 years after HTX (121 men; mean age, 46.2+/-11.3 years; observation period, 6.1+/-3.8 years) and correlated genotype to the onset and progression of CAVD, defined as luminal obstruction > 50%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "We found allelic frequencies to be 28.8% (n = 42) for ACE-DD, 49.3% (n = 72) for ACE-DI, and 21.9% (n = 32) for ACE-II. Differences in actuarial freedom from vasculopathy were significant 6 years after transplantation, with 84.6% for ACE-II compared with 54.4% for ACE-DD. We observed intermediate results for ACE-DI genotype (77.3%, p = 0.015)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In this large cohort study, we demonstrated a close relationship between the recipient-related ACE-D genotype and development of advanced CAVD. These observations suggest that gene-environment interactions might be clinically important in coronary vasculopathy after HTX."
        }
      ]},
